      "id": "3c011ea7-eda6-4f9a-94d9-59573c715cd3",
      "title": "whistleblower",
      "text": "Use this term discerningly. A whistleblower is a credible person from inside a company or the government who has firsthand knowledge or documentation of wrongdoing, then makes a disclosure with the reasonable belief that it shows evidence of lawbreaking, misconduct, abuse of power or unethical behavior. A whistleblower can raise concerns internally, make disclosures to an official entity \u2014 law enforcement, Congress, an inspector general, etc. \u2014 or alert the public, either directly or through journalists. The dozens of whistleblower protection laws at the federal and state level can vary in their definitions, but a common thread is that a whistleblower is someone exercising their First Amendment rights, through lawfully established procedures, to challenge an abuse that betrays the public trust. A whistleblower\u2019s motive can and should be taken into account for assessing their reliability, but the federal Whistleblower Protection Act specifically says it is irrelevant to the protections the person receives. The accuracy and credibility of the information provided is most important. ",
      "terms": [
        "whistleblower protection law",
        "whistleblower complaints",
        "whistleblower disclosure",
